msgid "**A stub that runs kernels in MoonBit on the JavaScript target.** It would bypass the C runtime and the ownership rules of the specification, giving results that the real backend might not reproduce."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/js.md:56
#: manual/design/backend/native.md:297
#: manual/design/core.md:90
#: manual/design/plan.md:134
#: manual/design/shared.md:116
#: manual/design/workflow.md:138
msgid "Boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/js.md:58
msgid "The package does not execute plans or workflows, declare foreign functions, load the Node.js addon, or move data across the JavaScript boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:1
msgid "backend/native design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:5
msgid "`backend/native` is where `luna_thread` actually runs code in parallel. It has two jobs: run data-parallel integer kernels on MoonBit arrays without copying them, and run a workflow graph's synchronisation protocol on real threads. Both are implemented in C, behind a narrow interface of integers and borrowed arrays, so that the same runtime can later serve the JavaScript addon."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:11
msgid "This page describes the threading model, the memory model and the mathematics of the kernels as they are implemented in `native/src/runtime.c` and the bridge `ffi_runtime_bridge.c`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:17
msgid "Chunked evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:19
msgid "Let $x = (x_0, \\dots, x_{n-1})$ be the input, $w$ the worker count and $c$ the chunk size, with $0 < w \\le n$ and $0 < c \\le n$. The runtime requires the **cover condition**"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:27
msgid "so that one worker can take each chunk, and splits $[0, n)$ into $k$ contiguous chunks $[s_j, s_{j+1})$ with"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:34
msgid "**Chunk sizes.** Every chunk has $\\lfloor n/k \\rfloor$ or $\\lceil n/k \\rceil$ elements, and therefore at most $c$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:46
msgid "The first claim is the usual balanced-partition argument: if the remaining $r = n - s_j$ elements and $m = k - j$ chunks satisfy $m \\lfloor n/k \\rfloor \\le r \\le m \\lceil n/k \\rceil$, then $\\lceil r/m \\rceil$ lies between $\\lfloor n/k \\rfloor$ and $\\lceil n/k \\rceil$, and the inequality holds again for $r - \\lceil r/m \\rceil$ and $m - 1$. At $j = 0$ it holds with $r = n$, $m = k$.[^cover]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:53
msgid "The code computes the chunk count as $\\min(k, w)$, which equals $k$ under the cover condition. Without the condition the chunks would be larger than $c$, so the runtime rejects it instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:57
msgid "Reductions as monoid folds"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:59
msgid "A reduction combines the elements with an associative operation $\\oplus$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:65
msgid "Sum, minimum and maximum on the integers are associative and commutative. Associativity is what makes chunking correct: with chunk results $P_j = x_{s_j} \\oplus \\dots \\oplus x_{s_{j+1}-1}$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:74
msgid "by the generalised associative law, whatever $w$ and $c$ are. Minimum and maximum seed each chunk with its first element, so they only need a semigroup; this is one reason the runtime requires $n > 0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:78
msgid "Prefix sums by blocks"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:80
msgid "The scan computes the inclusive prefix sums $y_i = \\sum_{t=0}^{i} x_t$ in three phases. With $S_j$ the sum of chunk $j$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:83
msgid "In parallel, each chunk computes its local prefix sums $L_j(i) = \\sum_{t=s_j}^{i} x_t$ for $s_j \\le i < s_{j+1}$, writing them to the output, and its total $S_j = L_j(s_{j+1} - 1)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:86
msgid "Sequentially, the carries $C_0 = 0$ and $C_j = C_{j-1} + S_{j-1}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:87
msgid "In parallel, each chunk adds its carry: $y_i = C_j + L_j(i)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:89
msgid "Phase 3 is correct because, by induction on $j$, $C_j = \\sum_{t=0}^{s_j - 1} x_t$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:100
msgid "The scan does $2n + k$ additions; with $k$ workers its critical path is $O(n/k + k)$, against $O(n)$ for the sequential loop."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:105
msgid "Checked integer arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:107
msgid "The kernels work on $\\mathbb{Z}/2^{32}$ and $\\mathbb{Z}/2^{64}$, where `+` wraps around. The runtime instead treats them as the integers and refuses to return a wrapped value: every addition $a + b$ is checked before it is made,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:116
msgid "and these tests do not overflow themselves. The map kernel ($x \\mapsto 2x$) first checks every element in one parallel pass and writes only if none overflows, so on failure the output is untouched."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:120
msgid "The consequence is that a successful result is exact: each machine addition that was performed had an in-range true result, so it equals the integer addition, and by induction the returned sum or prefix equals $\\sum x_t$ in $\\mathbb{Z}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:125
msgid "The cost is that checked addition is a partial operation and is **not** associative: whether some partial sum leaves the range depends on the bracketing. With $x = (-1, 0, \\mathrm{MAX}, 1)$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:136
msgid "So the values are independent of $w$ and $c$, but the set of accepted inputs is not. The alternative, wrapping arithmetic, would make every result defined and chunking-independent, but silently wrong as an integer; the runtime chose exactness."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:141
msgid "Fixed kernels in C"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:143
msgid "Plans can name `Map` and reduction kernels, but MoonBit closures cannot cross the C interface and run on C threads. The runtime therefore implements a fixed set of kernels in C: doubling, sum, minimum, maximum and prefix sum, for 32- and 64-bit integers. The facade wraps the 32-bit doubling, sum and prefix sum; the others are reachable through the `ffi_*` declarations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:149
msgid "Borrowed arrays, caller-allocated output"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:151
msgid "The MoonBit side passes `FixedArray` payloads to C with `#borrow`: C reads the input and writes the output in place and takes no ownership, so no copy is made and no reference count changes. The typed wrappers allocate the output with `FixedArray::make` before the call. Reductions return their value directly; the bridge passes a stack variable as the one-element output."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:157
msgid "One scheduler lock for workflows"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:159
msgid "The workflow runtime keeps all scheduling state, the ready queue, dependency counters, channel slots, mutex flags and barrier counters, under a single `pthread_mutex_t`, and workers execute each node while holding it. This makes every node step atomic with respect to the others without any per-capability locks, at the price that non-compute nodes never run simultaneously; they are bookkeeping steps that take constant time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:166
msgid "The threading model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:170
msgid "Each kernel runs its chunk loop as an OpenMP `parallel for` with `num_threads(w)` when the runtime is compiled with OpenMP, one iteration per chunk. Without OpenMP the pragmas are ignored and `select_thread_count` returns $1$, so the same chunks run one after another on the calling thread. The `moon` build compiles the native stubs without OpenMP flags, so from MoonBit the kernels currently run sequentially; the CMake build of `native/` enables OpenMP. Because the chunking is the same in both builds, the results and the overflow behaviour are the same."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:181
msgid "`submit_workflow_async` starts $w + 1$ POSIX threads: $w$ workers and one **compute lane**. Scheduling is Kahn's topological sort run by a pool:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:184
msgid "Each node $v$ has a counter of unfinished predecessors, initially its in-degree. Nodes with counter $0$ go into a FIFO ring buffer of capacity $|V|$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:187
msgid "A worker waits on the scheduler's condition variable until the queue is non-empty, dequeues a node and executes it. Completing a node decrements the counters of its successors and enqueues those that reach $0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:190
msgid "A compute node is handed to the compute lane, and the worker waits for it. In v1 the lane only checks the node kind and reports success; the plan is not executed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:193
msgid "When all $|V|$ nodes have completed the state becomes `Completed`; the first failing node sets `Failed`, its status and `failed_node_id`. Both wake every waiting thread so that the pool shuts down."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:197
msgid "There are no per-thread queues and no work stealing: all workers share one queue, and FIFO order with execution under the lock makes the order in which ready nodes run the order in which they became ready."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:201
msgid "Node semantics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:203
msgid "Node"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:203
msgid "Effect when executed"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:205
msgid "Completes immediately; no data moves."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:206
msgid "Blocks if the channel slot is full, otherwise fills it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:207
msgid "Blocks if the slot is empty, otherwise empties it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:208
msgid "Blocks if the mutex flag is set, otherwise sets it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:209
msgid "Fails with status 12 if the flag is clear, otherwise clears it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:210
msgid "Blocks until a `Signal` on the same condition variable wakes it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:211
msgid "Wakes one blocked `Wait`, if any; otherwise the signal is lost."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:212
msgid "Blocks until every barrier node of its group has arrived."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:214
msgid "A channel is a slot of capacity one, and capabilities carry no payload: the runtime enforces the protocol, not the transfer of data."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:217
msgid "A barrier's group is the set of `Barrier` nodes with the same capability and the same depth $d(v)$, the length of the longest path from a source to $v$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:224
msgid "The runtime computes $d$ by $|V|$ rounds of relaxation over all edges, which is enough because a longest path in an acyclic graph has at most $|V| - 1$ edges. A group with fewer than two nodes fails with status 14 (`BARRIER_BROKEN`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:228
msgid "The memory model"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:230
msgid "For kernels, the chunks partition $[0, n)$, and in each parallel phase iteration $j$ writes only output indices in $[s_j, s_{j+1})$ and its own `partials[j]` or `summaries[j]` cell. Distinct iterations therefore write disjoint locations, and there is no data race. The end of an OpenMP `parallel for` is a barrier, so the sequential carry phase sees every phase-one write, and phase three sees the carries. The MoonBit caller is blocked for the whole call, so no MoonBit code touches the arrays while C threads run, and the borrowed arrays stay alive because the caller still holds them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:240
msgid "For workflows, every access to shared scheduler state happens under the scheduler mutex, and condition-variable waits re-acquire it, so all such accesses are ordered. `poll_workflow` takes the same mutex to copy a consistent snapshot."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:247
msgid "**Kernel results.** When a kernel succeeds, the doubled values, the sum and the prefix sums equal their values in $\\mathbb{Z}$, independent of $w$ and $c$, as derived above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:250
msgid "**Validation before work.** Every kernel checks null pointers, positive sizes, $w \\le n$, $c \\le n$, buffer lengths and the cover condition before it allocates or computes, and returns status 1 otherwise (7 for a null pointer)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:254
msgid "**Workflow admission.** Before starting threads the runtime requires a positive worker and node count, capabilities for the nodes that need them with matching kinds, no `RwLock`, `Semaphore` or `Opaque` capability, no self edge, existing edge endpoints, and acyclicity, which it checks completely by Kahn's algorithm: a finite graph is acyclic exactly when repeatedly removing nodes without incoming edges removes them all."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:260
msgid "**Termination.** If no `Send`, `Recv` or `Lock` node ever blocks, every `Wait` is signalled after it blocks, and every barrier group has at least two nodes, every node completes once and the workflow reaches `Completed` or `Failed`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:265
msgid "Known defects"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:267
msgid "These behaviours of the current branch contradict the intent of the code and are tracked for fixing:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:270
msgid "**Request lifetime.** `luna_mbt_workflow_submit_async` builds the request on its stack and frees the capability, node and edge arrays right after the runtime starts, but the worker threads keep a pointer to that request. Workflow runs read freed memory and crash intermittently."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:274
msgid "**Lost wake-ups.** Only `Signal` and a completed barrier re-enqueue blocked nodes. A `Send`, `Recv` or `Lock` that blocks is never retried, so the workflow never finishes and `wait_workflow` does not return."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:277
msgid "**Errors without a channel.** A rejected workflow becomes an empty handle reported as `Ok`, and `execute_reduce_sum_i32` reports failures as `0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:279
msgid "**Runtime identity.** `supports_openmp()` is the constant `true`, while the `moon` build has no OpenMP."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:284
msgid "**Work-stealing deques.** Per-worker deques pay off when tasks are many and uneven. Workflow nodes here are constant-time protocol steps executed under one lock, so a shared FIFO is simpler and enough; data parallelism is left to OpenMP inside each kernel."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:288
msgid "**Executing MoonBit closures on C threads.** The MoonBit runtime gives no guarantee that its objects may be used from foreign threads, so the kernels are fixed C functions instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:291
msgid "**Copying arrays across the interface.** Copies would make the memory model trivial but double the memory traffic of every kernel; borrowing plus disjoint writes gives the same safety."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:294
msgid "**Wrapping arithmetic.** Rejected in favour of exact results, as derived above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/native.md:299
msgid "The backend does not execute plans, user-defined kernels, floating-point data or `Bytes`; it does not move data through channels or shared capabilities; it does not implement read-write locks or semaphores; it does not detect deadlocks or offer timeouts; and it does not build for any target but `native`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:1
msgid "core design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:5
msgid "The root package is the facade most programs import. It should let a program build policies, plans and workflows, run kernels and submit workflows through one import, with sensible defaults, while the real definitions stay in the packages that own them. It is also where the backend is chosen, so it is the only package besides `backend/native` that depends on the C runtime."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:13
msgid "The facade adds no new concepts; it is a set of equations between its functions and those of the underlying packages. With $\\mathbf{d}$ the default policy $(\\mathrm{Native}, \\mathrm{Synchronous}, 1, 1, \\mathrm{PreserveInputOrder})$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:25
msgid "and likewise for every other function. The only facts specific to the facade are its defaults. For the kernels the defaults are $w = c = 1$, and the native cover condition $w \\ge \\lceil n / c \\rceil$ becomes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:33
msgid "so with default arguments the kernels accept only inputs of length one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:37
msgid "Free functions with defaults"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:39
msgid "The facade exposes free functions such as `map`, `workflow` and `spawn_task` rather than methods on the imported types, because the types belong to other packages and cannot gain methods here. Optional arguments carry the defaults, so the common call is short: `@luna_thread.map(\"double\", @luna_thread.i32_type(), 16)` is a complete, valid plan. Capability helpers bind each kind to its only valid access mode (`channel_capability` to `MoveOnly`, `mutex_capability` to `SynchronizeOnly`, `shared_read_capability` to `ReadOnly`), which removes a whole class of `InvalidCapabilityAccess` issues."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:48
msgid "Backend routing in one place"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:50
msgid "`submit_workflow` dispatches on the backend: `Native` goes to `@native.submit_workflow`, anything else to `@workflow.submit`. Both produce the same validated submission today, but routing through the backend package is where execution will be attached. The direct-execution and asynchronous functions call `backend/native` unconditionally, because it is the only backend that executes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:57
msgid "The facade is native-only"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:59
msgid "Because it imports `backend/native`, whose foreign functions exist only on the native target, the facade declares `supported_targets = \"native\"`. Code that must build everywhere imports `plan`, `shared` and `workflow` directly; they provide everything except execution."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:64
msgid "Kernel defaults kept from the original interface"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:66
msgid "The kernel defaults $w = c = 1$ are the smallest valid policy values, matching `make_policy`, but as derived above they satisfy the cover condition only for one element. Changing them would change the meaning of existing calls; the API and tutorial pages tell users to pass both arguments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:73
msgid "**Faithful forwarding.** Each facade function returns exactly what the underlying function returns for the same arguments and defaults; the facade holds no state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:76
msgid "**Defaults are valid policies.** `default_policy()` lies in the v1 policy subset; `make_policy` with no arguments returns `Ok(default_policy())`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:78
msgid "**Single dependency on C.** Only `execute_*`, `submit_workflow_async`, `poll_workflow`, `wait_workflow` and `drop_workflow` reach the C runtime."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:83
msgid "**Re-exporting whole packages.** Re-exporting every name of `plan`, `shared` and `workflow` would duplicate their API pages; the facade keeps the entry points a typical program needs, and the packages remain importable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:86
msgid "**Choosing the backend by target at compile time.** That would make the facade build on every target but silently change behaviour with the target; v1 makes the native requirement explicit instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:92
msgid "The facade does not define types, validate anything itself, execute plans, manage thread pools, or support the JavaScript backend; it forwards to the packages that do or will."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:1
msgid "plan design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:5
msgid "A plan states *what* data-parallel operation to perform, on what input shape, under which policy, without saying *how* or running it. Keeping this as plain, comparable data lets `luna_thread` validate work before it reaches a runtime, embed it in workflows, and translate it for different backends. The package must stay backend-independent and build on every target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:13
msgid "Let the input be a sequence $x = (x_0, \\dots, x_{n-1}) \\in A^n$ with $n > 0$. The four plan kinds denote these functions:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:25
msgid "A reduction kernel must be **associative**, $(a \\oplus b) \\oplus c = a \\oplus (b \\oplus c)$, so that the bracketing a parallel runtime chooses does not matter. The three kernels of v1 are commutative and associative on the integers:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:29
msgid "Kernel"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:29
msgid "Identity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:32
msgid "none in $\\mathbb{Z}$; the largest value of a fixed-width type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:33
msgid "none in $\\mathbb{Z}$; the smallest value of a fixed-width type"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:37
msgid "Plans are data, not closures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:39
msgid "The plan records the kind, the element type, the length, the policy, an optional named kernel and the ordering, and nothing executable. A closure could express any $f$ and $\\oplus$, but it cannot be compared, inspected, validated, or passed to C threads. Named kernels can be checked against what a runtime implements: `reduction_kernel_is_supported_in_v1` accepts exactly the kernels with a C implementation, and `Custom(name)` keeps a place for kernels that a later runtime may register."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:47
msgid "Ordering is a property of the kind"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:49
msgid "`OrderingGuarantee` says whether results must keep input positions. For a reduction with a commutative and associative $\\oplus$, the result is the same for every permutation $\\pi$ of the input:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:57
msgid "by the generalised commutative law, so `RelaxedOrder` is safe for `Reduce` and `MapReduce`, and for `Map` it only permits results to be produced out of order. A scan is different: $y_i$ depends on which elements come before position $i$. For $x = (1, 2)$ and the swap $\\pi$, the sums are"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:66
msgid "so a scan has no meaning without input order. `scan` therefore always sets `PreserveInputOrder`, and `validate` reports both `UnsupportedOrdering` and `ScanRequiresStableOrdering` for a hand-built relaxed scan."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:70
msgid "The v1 subset is checked, not encoded in types"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:72
msgid "`ValueType` lists `F32`, `F64`, `Bytes` and `Opaque`, and `ExecutionPolicy` can name the JavaScript backend and asynchronous mode, although v1 supports none of them. The types describe the whole model of the specification; `validate` decides what the current runtime accepts. When a runtime grows, the predicates change and user code that builds plans does not."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:78
msgid "Integer element types are also the ones for which chunked reduction is well-defined. Floating-point addition is not associative: in `Double`,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:85
msgid "so a parallel sum of `F64` data would depend on the worker count and chunk size. Supporting it needs a decision about which bracketing to promise, which v1 does not make."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:89
msgid "Validation reports every issue"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:91
msgid "`validate` returns an array of all issues rather than the first one, so a caller can show every problem of a plan together, and a workflow can wrap each one as `InvalidComputePlan`. The issues are independent tests, listed in a fixed order. `is_runnable` is the conjunction of their negations:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:100
msgid "Size checks stop short of the cover condition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:102
msgid "`validate` rejects $c > n$ and $w > n$ because no runtime can use more chunks than elements. It does not check the native cover condition $w \\ge \\lceil n / c \\rceil$, which belongs to one backend's chunking strategy (see the [native backend design](backend/native.md)). Backends check their own conditions when they build requests or run kernels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:110
msgid "**Builders fix the kind.** `map`, `reduce`, `scan` and `map_reduce` produce plans of the corresponding kind; `reduce` and `map_reduce` always carry a kernel; `scan` and `reduce` always preserve order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:113
msgid "**Validation is total and pure.** `validate` terminates for every plan, has no effects, and depends only on the plan's fields."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:115
msgid "**Supported plans name implemented kernels.** If $\\operatorname{validate}(p) = [\\,]$ and $p$ reduces, its kernel is `Sum`, `Min` or `Max` and its element type is `I32` or `I64`, the combinations the C runtime implements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:119
msgid "**Equality is structural.** Two plans are equal exactly when all fields are equal, so plans can serve as keys and test expectations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:124
msgid "**A generic `Plan[T]`.** Typing the element would push the element type into every workflow node and backend signature, and the C interface would still need a runtime tag. A `ValueType` tag keeps plans of different element types in one array."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:128
msgid "**Separate types per kind.** `MapPlan`, `ReducePlan` and so on would encode \"reduce has a kernel\" in the type, but workflows and requests would need a sum type over them anyway; `PlanKind` plus validation is that sum type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:131
msgid "**Validation inside the builders.** Builders that return `Result` would make the hand-built plans that tests and tools need impossible to express."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/plan.md:136
msgid "The package does not execute plans, hold input data, allocate buffers, choose chunk sizes, check backend-specific conditions, or describe user-defined functions; the map function is implied by the backend (doubling in the native backend), not stored in the plan."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:1
msgid "shared design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:5
msgid "Every package of `luna_thread` needs to say which backend, which mode, how many workers and what ordering it means, and every backend needs to speak the C runtime's integer codes. `shared` is the one place where these are defined, so that `plan`, `workflow` and the backends agree on them without depending on each other. It has no dependencies of its own and builds on every target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:13
msgid "Policies as a refined product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:15
msgid "An execution policy is an element of the product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:21
msgid "backend, mode, worker count, chunk size and ordering. The v1 runtime accepts the subset"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:28
msgid "`make_execution_policy` is a partial constructor $P \\rightharpoonup P_1$: it returns its argument when it lies in $P_1$ and otherwise the first violated condition, in the order listed. `validate_policy` returns the list of all violated conditions, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:37
msgid "Status codes as a section and a retraction"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:39
msgid "Let $S$ be the eight constructors of `NativeStatus` and $\\gamma = $ `native_status_code` $: S \\to \\mathbb{Z}$ number them $0$ to $7$. Let $\\rho = $ `native_status_from_code` $: \\mathbb{Z} \\to S$ invert $\\gamma$ on $\\{0, \\dots, 7\\}$ and send every other integer to `InvalidArgument`. Then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:48
msgid "so $\\gamma$ is injective (a section) and $\\rho$ is surjective (a retraction). The other composite is the identity only on the image of $\\gamma$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:59
msgid "Converting a C status to `NativeStatus` and back therefore preserves codes $0$ to $7$ and collapses the workflow statuses $8$ to $14$ to $1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:64
msgid "Return the first policy error, list all policy issues"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:66
msgid "Constructing a policy is the common case, and one error message is enough to fix a call, so `make_execution_policy` returns `Result` with the first problem. Re-checking a stored policy happens inside workflow validation, which reports everything at once, so `validate_policy` returns all problems. Both test the same four conditions in the same order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:72
msgid "Aborting conveniences are kept small"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:74
msgid "`ExecutionPolicy::new`, `native_policy` and `javascript_policy` unwrap the result of `make_execution_policy`. For the native defaults this cannot fail. For the JavaScript backend it always fails in v1, so `javascript_policy` aborts; it exists so that code written for the planned JavaScript backend compiles today. Code that takes policy arguments from users should call `make_execution_policy`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:81
msgid "Capabilities are declared, not probed"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:83
msgid "`RuntimeCapabilities::for_backend` returns a fixed table. Probing the running system would need the native backend, which `shared` must not depend on, and would differ between the `moon` build and the CMake build. The table states the intended capabilities of each backend; the backend pages state what the current implementation does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:89
msgid "Integer-coded mirrors of the C structs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:91
msgid "`NativeBuffer` and the three request records copy the field order and integer encodings of the C structs in `luna_thread_runtime.h`, so that a binding can fill them without knowing the typed records of `backend/native`. The specification calls this correspondence the ABI layout isomorphism. The records store values unchecked; checking belongs to the backend that sends them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:99
msgid "**Policy subset.** Every policy returned by `make_execution_policy`, `ExecutionPolicy::new` or `native_policy` lies in $P_1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:101
msgid "**Status round trip.** $\\rho(\\gamma(s)) = s$ for every `NativeStatus` $s$, as derived above; the test in the [shared API](../api/shared.md) checks all eight."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:103
msgid "**No dependencies.** The package imports only the MoonBit core library, so the dependency graph of the module stays acyclic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:108
msgid "**A typed error per backend.** One `PolicyIssue` type keeps policies backend-independent; the issues name the unsupported backend or mode as data."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:110
msgid "**A partial `native_status_from_code`.** Returning `NativeStatus?` would force every caller to handle codes the runtime may add later; mapping them to `InvalidArgument` errs on the side of failure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:113
msgid "**Defining the integer codes in each backend.** The codes are part of the C contract, so they live in the package every backend shares."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/shared.md:118
msgid "The package does not run anything, probe the system, validate the integer request records, or describe statuses $8$ to $14$ as constructors; the workflow runtime reports those as raw integers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:1
msgid "workflow design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:5
msgid "Data-parallel plans cover one operation at a time. Real programs also spawn and join tasks, pass work through channels, and protect shared state with locks. The `workflow` package gives these a single description: a task graph whose nodes are typed steps, whose resources are explicit capabilities, and whose edges fix the order of execution. The graph is data, so it can be validated in MoonBit on any target before a runtime executes it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:14
msgid "A workflow is a triple $W = (C, V, E)$ of capabilities, nodes and edges."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:16
msgid "Each capability $\\kappa \\in C$ has an id, a kind $k(\\kappa) \\in K$ and an access mode $a(\\kappa) \\in M$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:18
msgid "Each node $v \\in V$ has an id, a kind $t(v) \\in T$ and optionally a capability $\\operatorname{cap}(v)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:20
msgid "Each edge $(u, v) \\in E$ says that $v$ may start only after $u$ has completed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:23
msgid "Two relations type the graph. $\\mathrm{Acc} \\subseteq K \\times M$ lists the valid access modes of each capability kind, and $\\mathrm{Use} \\subseteq T \\times K$ the capability kinds each node kind may use (both tables are in the [workflow API](../api/workflow.md)). A workflow is **well formed** when ids are unique, edges join existing distinct nodes, and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:35
msgid "The edges define the relation $u \\prec v$, \"there is a path from $u$ to $v$\". An execution order is a sequence of all nodes in which $u$ comes before $v$ whenever $u \\prec v$, a **topological order**. Such an order exists exactly when $(V, E)$ has no directed cycle: a cycle $v_0 \\to \\dots \\to v_m = v_0$ would require $v_0$ before itself, and conversely Kahn's algorithm builds an order for every acyclic graph by repeatedly removing a node without incoming edges."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:45
msgid "Capabilities are declared, nodes refer to them by id"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:47
msgid "Synchronisation resources exist independently of the steps that use them: two nodes that send and receive must agree on one channel. Declaring a capability once and referring to it by id makes that agreement explicit and checkable, and gives the runtime a dense table of resources to allocate. The access mode records the intent of the declaration, such as `MoveOnly` for a channel or `ReadOnly` for a shared view, so that `validate` can reject a `WriteShared` node on a read-only buffer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:55
msgid "Every edge kind orders the same way"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:57
msgid "`DataDependency`, `ControlDependency`, `OwnershipTransfer` and `SynchronizationDependency` all mean $u$ completes before $v$ starts. The kind documents why the edge exists, and the specification gives ownership transfer further meaning, but v1 checks and schedules all edges alike, so the order relation $\\prec$ does not depend on the kinds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:63
msgid "Builders mutate in place"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:65
msgid "`Workflow` stores its capabilities, nodes and edges in `Array`s, and `add_capability`, `add_node` and `add_edge` push onto them and return the same workflow. This makes chained construction cheap, $O(1)$ amortised per step, but it is not a persistent builder: after `let b = a.add_node(n)`, `a` and `b` are the same value and both contain `n`. Code that needs two variants must build two workflows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:72
msgid "Validation is layered"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:74
msgid "`validate` in MoonBit checks the whole data model on every target: policy, capability kinds and access modes, node and capability references, edge endpoints, the plans of compute nodes, and cycles. The native runtime checks again what it relies on when a workflow is submitted. The two layers agree on most rules but not all:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:80
msgid "Rule"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:80
msgid "Native runtime"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:82
msgid "`RwLock`, `Semaphore` capabilities"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:82
#: manual/design/workflow.md:83
msgid "accepted"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:82
msgid "rejected (status 8)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:83
msgid "`Lock` and `Unlock` on an `RwLock`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:83
msgid "rejected"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:84
msgid "Access modes, duplicate ids, compute plans"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:84
msgid "checked"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:84
msgid "not checked"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:85
msgid "Cycles"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:85
msgid "partial (below)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:85
msgid "complete (status 9)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:87
msgid "The MoonBit layer reports every issue, so a user sees all problems at once; the native layer stops at the first, because it only decides whether to start."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:90
msgid "The MoonBit cycle check is partial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:92
msgid "`validate` reports `CyclicDependency` when (a) the graph has nodes but none without incoming edges, or (b) two edges point in opposite directions between the same pair of nodes. Both conditions imply a cycle when every edge joins existing nodes:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:97
msgid "(b) is a cycle of length two."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:98
msgid "For (a), walk backwards from any node along incoming edges. Every node has one, so the walk never stops; with $|V|$ nodes it must repeat a node within $|V| + 1$ steps, and the repeated segment is a cycle."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:102
msgid "The converse fails: in $1 \\to 2 \\to 3 \\to 4 \\to 2$, node $1$ has no incoming edge and no pair of edges is opposite, so `validate` reports nothing although $2 \\to 3 \\to 4 \\to 2$ is a cycle. The native runtime runs Kahn's algorithm, which removes $1$ and then finds no node to remove among $\\{2, 3, 4\\}$, and rejects the graph. An edge from an unknown node also counts as an incoming edge for (a), so a graph whose only incoming edges come from unknown ids gets a `CyclicDependency` next to its `InvalidNodeReference` issues."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:112
msgid "**Soundness of acceptance.** If `validate` returns no issue, the workflow is well formed in the sense above, except possibly for a cycle of length three or more that is reachable from a source."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:115
msgid "**Soundness of rejection.** Every issue names a real violation, with the one `CyclicDependency` caveat for edges from unknown nodes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:117
msgid "**Submission.** `submit(w, backend)` is accepted exactly when `validate(w)` is empty and `backend` is `Native`; it never sets `completed`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:119
msgid "**Complexity.** `validate` runs in $O(|V|^2 + |C|^2 + |E|^2 + |V| \\cdot |C| + |V| \\cdot |E|)$: duplicate detection filters the whole array for each element, endpoint and in-degree lookups scan the node and edge arrays, and the opposite-edge test compares all pairs of edges."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:127
msgid "**Capabilities embedded in nodes.** A channel stored inside each `Send` node could not be shared with the matching `Recv`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:129
msgid "**Typed edges with different scheduling.** Giving each edge kind its own scheduling rule would make the order relation depend on runtime policy; v1 keeps one rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:132
msgid "**A persistent builder.** Copying the arrays on every `add_*` would make construction quadratic for the convenience of keeping old versions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:134
msgid "**Full cycle detection in MoonBit.** A topological sort in `validate` would close the gap described above; the current check is cheaper and the native runtime catches the remaining cases. The gap is a candidate for a fix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/workflow.md:140
msgid "The package does not schedule or execute workflows, move data between nodes, check that blocking nodes are matched (that a `Recv` has a `Send` that runs first), detect deadlocks, or give edge kinds different meanings."
msgstr ""

